WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.967
Structured data follows a stable arrangement.

00:00:02.967 --> 00:00:10.157
Relational databases are a classic example because each row follows a known set of columns and types.

00:00:10.157 --> 00:00:14.612
CSV and Parquet files can also contain structured data.

00:00:14.612 --> 00:00:20.492
The benefit is predictability. We can validate and query fields consistently.

00:00:20.492 --> 00:00:23.122
But structured does not mean correct.

00:00:23.122 --> 00:00:27.477
A structured table can still contain duplicates, missing values, inconsistent categories,

00:00:27.477 --> 00:00:31.187
or impossible measurements.

00:00:31.187 --> 00:00:38.222
A useful project question is: what preparation step is required before analysis can even begin?

00:00:38.222 --> 00:00:41.592
A relational table may be ready for profiling, JSON may need flattening,

00:00:41.592 --> 00:00:47.612
and free text may need NLP or another extraction step.

00:00:47.612 --> 00:00:52.972
The form of the data changes the engineering path, but it does not by itself tell us whether

00:00:52.972 --> 00:00:55.040
the data is high quality.
